WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.800
Welcome to the complete guide to Solitaire.

2
00:00:02.867 --> 00:00:05.280
If you've ever opened a deck of cards, looked at the board,

3
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:08.400
and wondered what you're actually supposed to do, you're in the right place.

4
00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:11.228
Solitaire is one of those games that looks simple at first.

5
00:00:11.248 --> 00:00:14.500
You move cards around, build a few piles, and try to clear the board.

6
00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:15.676
But once you start playing,

7
00:00:15.696 --> 00:00:19.000
you quickly realize there's a lot more strategy involved than you might expect.

8
00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:22.366
Every move can reveal new information, open up new possibilities,

9
00:00:22.386 --> 00:00:24.633
or accidentally block a card you need later.

10
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.546
And that's what makes Solitaire interesting.

11
00:00:26.566 --> 00:00:27.775
You're not just moving cards.

12
00:00:27.795 --> 00:00:30.000
You're making decisions with incomplete information.

13
00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:32.232
In this guide, we'll start from the very beginning.

14
00:00:32.252 --> 00:00:35.747
We'll look at the board, explain every important rule, walk through real examples,

15
00:00:35.767 --> 00:00:38.767
and show you how to think about your moves instead of simply guessing.

16
00:00:38.833 --> 00:00:41.077
We'll also cover how Solitaire works on GRYND,

17
00:00:41.097 --> 00:00:42.967
including its competitive Duel format.

18
00:00:43.033 --> 00:00:45.809
By the end, you'll understand how to play, what to look for,

19
00:00:45.829 --> 00:00:47.367
and how to make better decisions.

20
00:00:47.433 --> 00:00:48.200
Let's get started.

21
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.533
First, let's understand what we're looking at.

22
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:55.267
We're playing Klondike Solitaire, the classic version most people recognize.

23
00:00:55.333 --> 00:00:57.994
A standard game uses a deck of 52 cards.

24
00:00:58.014 --> 00:01:01.867
There are four suits: hearts, diamonds, clubs, and spades.

25
00:01:01.933 --> 00:01:03.288
Hearts and diamonds are red.

26
00:01:03.308 --> 00:01:04.633
Clubs and spades are black.

27
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:07.370
Each suit has thirteen cards, starting with Ace,

28
00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:11.167
followed by the numbers two through ten, then Jack, Queen, and King.

29
00:01:11.233 --> 00:01:12.367
Now, look at the board.

30
00:01:12.433 --> 00:01:14.367
There are four main areas you need to understand.

31
00:01:14.433 --> 00:01:18.067
At the top, you'll find the stock, the waste, and the four foundation piles.

32
00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:20.333
The stock is the pile of cards you haven't drawn yet.

33
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.219
When you draw a card from the stock, it moves to the waste pile.

34
00:01:23.239 --> 00:01:26.300
The waste is where you can access the currently available drawn card.

35
00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:28.523
Beside those piles are the four foundations.

36
00:01:28.543 --> 00:01:30.933
Each foundation represents one of the four suits.

37
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.417
Below everything is the tableau.

38
00:01:32.437 --> 00:01:35.400
That's the main playing area, where most of your decisions happen.

39
00:01:35.467 --> 00:01:36.933
The tableau has seven columns.

40
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.156
The first column contains one card, the second contains two,

41
00:01:40.176 --> 00:01:43.384
the third contains three, and so on until the seventh column,

42
00:01:43.404 --> 00:01:44.833
which contains seven cards.

43
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:46.767
That gives us twenty-eight cards in the tableau.

44
00:01:46.833 --> 00:01:49.053
Only the top card of each column starts face-up.

45
00:01:49.073 --> 00:01:50.567
The cards underneath are hidden.

46
00:01:50.633 --> 00:01:52.933
The remaining twenty-four cards go into the stock.

47
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.967
So, at the beginning, you have a combination of visible and hidden information.

48
00:01:57.033 --> 00:01:57.952
And that's important,

49
00:01:57.972 --> 00:02:01.200
because revealing hidden cards is one of the main ways you make progress.

50
00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:03.533
Before moving on, remember these four areas:

51
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.000
The stock gives you new cards.

52
00:02:05.067 --> 00:02:07.433
The waste gives you access to the latest drawn card.

53
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.100
The tableau is where you organize and uncover cards.

54
00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:12.533
And the foundations are where you build completed suits.

55
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.367
Once you understand those areas, the rest of the game becomes much easier to follow.

56
00:02:17.767 --> 00:02:21.533
Now let's look at the most important rule in Solitaire: moving cards in the tableau.

57
00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.300
Cards in the tableau must be arranged in descending order.

58
00:02:24.367 --> 00:02:27.800
That means you place a lower-ranked card on top of a card that is one rank higher.

59
00:02:27.867 --> 00:02:28.967
But there's another condition.

60
00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:30.300
The colors must alternate.

61
00:02:30.367 --> 00:02:32.597
So, a red card can be placed on a black card,

62
00:02:32.617 --> 00:02:34.867
and a black card can be placed on a red card.

63
00:02:34.933 --> 00:02:37.567
For example, a black eight can receive a red seven.

64
00:02:37.633 --> 00:02:38.900
A red queen can receive a black jack.

65
00:02:38.967 --> 00:02:40.152
But you cannot place a red seven on another red eight.

66
00:02:40.172 --> 00:02:41.267
The colors are the same, so that move is illegal.

67
00:02:41.333 --> 00:02:44.567
Think of the rule as two conditions that must always be satisfied:

68
00:02:44.633 --> 00:02:47.300
One: the new card must be exactly one rank lower.

69
00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:50.100
Two: its color must be different from the card underneath it.

70
00:02:50.167 --> 00:02:51.567
Both conditions must be true.

71
00:02:51.633 --> 00:02:52.700
Now, here's something useful.

72
00:02:52.767 --> 00:02:54.633
You don't always have to move just one card.

73
00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.736
If you have a face-up sequence that already follows the descending,

74
00:02:57.756 --> 00:03:00.767
alternating-color rule, you can move the entire sequence together.

75
00:03:00.833 --> 00:03:04.567
For example, imagine a black ten with a red nine and a black eight beneath it.

76
00:03:04.633 --> 00:03:05.900
That is a valid sequence.

77
00:03:05.967 --> 00:03:09.633
If you find a red jack in another column, you can move the entire sequence onto it.

78
00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:13.594
This is important because moving complete sequences can reorganize the board and

79
00:03:13.614 --> 00:03:15.033
create space for other cards.

80
00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.333
But remember: you can only move face-up cards.

81
00:03:17.353 --> 00:03:19.900
Hidden cards cannot be moved until they're revealed.

82
00:03:19.967 --> 00:03:22.267
There's also one special rule for empty columns.

83
00:03:22.333 --> 00:03:26.233
If a tableau column becomes completely empty, only a King can be placed there.

84
00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:30.000
You cannot use an empty column for a queen, a jack, or any other card.

85
00:03:30.067 --> 00:03:32.733
Kings are the only cards that can start a new tableau column.

86
00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:34.567
And that makes empty columns valuable.

87
00:03:34.633 --> 00:03:37.442
They give you room to reorganize the board, move sequences,

88
00:03:37.462 --> 00:03:39.667
and expose cards that were previously blocked.

89
00:03:39.733 --> 00:03:43.367
So, whenever you see an empty column, think carefully before filling it.

90
00:03:43.433 --> 00:03:45.710
It can be tempting to place the first King you find,

91
00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:48.733
but sometimes waiting for the right King gives you more flexibility.
